ABSTRACT

The agent needs memory. It must remember what it decided, why, and what happened. It must also remain deterministic: the same inputs must produce the same outputs, forever. These two requirements are in tension. Memory that can be rewritten introduces nondeterminism. Memory that is read-only cannot support an agent.

A7 resolves the tension with a two-layer memory model:

1. The Append-Only Log (AOL) — immutable, signed, complete history. Never rewritten.
2. The Bounded Working Set (BWS) — a fixed-size window of recent state, deterministic in its update rule.

The AOL is the agent's past. The BWS is the agent's present. Neither is rewritable. Together they preserve determinism across the agent's entire lifetime.

Status: Specification. No reference implementation. No hardware validation. Machine verification pending.

