1. INTRODUCTION: WHY MEMORY IS DANGEROUS

1.1 The Determinism Requirement

Corollary 5 of the Verifiable Agency Theorem states: given a fixed input sequence, the agent produces exactly one trace.

Any mechanism that introduces state-dependent variation across identical input sequences breaks this. Rewritable memory is such a mechanism.

1.2 What Agents Need From Memory

Need Without it
Recall prior decisions The agent repeats mistakes or contradicts itself
Audit its own history No certification trail
Support rollback (A3 §7.3) Checkpoints are impossible
Maintain context across tasks Each task starts from zero
Support tile invocation (A6 §7) Stack frames have nowhere to live

1.3 The Tension

Requirement Mechanism Cost
Remember Mutable memory Nondeterminism risk
Determinism Immutable memory No adaptation

A7 resolves this by separating the two. History is immutable. Present state is bounded. Neither is free-form mutable.

1.4 The Design Principle

Memory is a log, not a scratchpad. State is a window, not a heap. Nothing is ever erased; nothing is ever rewritten.

---

2. THE TWO-LAYER MODEL

2.1 Definition

Definition 1 (Memory Model). The agent's memory consists of two layers:

```
Memory = ⟨ AOL, BWS ⟩
```

Layer Persistence Mutability Purpose
AOL — Append-Only Log Lifetime Immutable Complete history
BWS — Bounded Working Set Per-task Window-only Recent context

The AOL grows monotonically. The BWS slides deterministically.

2.2 The SRAM/ROM Split, Revisited

The base standard (standard §2.2) specifies 75% ROM / 25% SRAM. A7 assigns semantics:

· ROM (75%) — immutable, holds constants, burned facts, plan library, action library.
· SRAM (25%) — holds the AOL tail and the BWS.
· External persistent storage (if present) — holds the AOL archive, if the deployment requires it.

The split is unchanged. A7 specifies how the SRAM portion is used.

2.3 Finiteness

Proposition A7-F (Finiteness). Both AOL and BWS are finite at any time step.

Proof. The AOL is bounded by the total number of transitions the agent can take, which is bounded by T_AET × N_tasks for a finite task count. The BWS is bounded by declared size W (A4 §2.1). Both are finite. □

---

3. THE APPEND-ONLY LOG

3.1 Definition

Definition 2 (AOL Entry). An AOL entry is a tuple:

```
entry = ⟨ seq, timestamp, transition, prev_hash, entry_hash, signature ⟩
```

Field Type Description
seq Nat Monotonic sequence number
timestamp Nat Deterministic cycle counter value
transition TransitionRecord What happened
prev_hash Hash Hash of the previous entry
entry_hash Hash Hash of this entry
signature SK-1024 sig Signature over the entry

3.2 Transition Record

Definition 3 (TransitionRecord). The transition field is one of:

```
TransitionRecord ::=
  | StateTransition ⟨ prev_state_hash, action_id, next_state_hash ⟩
  | InvocationStart ⟨ caller, callee, task_id, ctx_hash ⟩
  | InvocationEnd   ⟨ caller, callee, result_hash, status ⟩
  | Refusal         ⟨ reason_code, action_id ⟩
  | Error           ⟨ error_code, context_hash ⟩
  | Checkpoint      ⟨ state_hash, label ⟩
```

Every record type is finite. Every record is signed.

3.3 Append-Only Rule

Rule AOL1 (No Modification). Once written, an entry is never modified, deleted, or reordered.

Rule AOL2 (No Gaps). seq values are consecutive integers starting from 0.

Rule AOL3 (Hash Chain). prev_hash of entry n+1 equals entry_hash of entry n.

Rule AOL4 (Deterministic Append). The entry is written by the FSM at a declared state. The write is not interruptible.

3.4 The Hash Chain Property

Proposition A7-H (Tamper Evidence). Any modification to any entry in the AOL is detectable.

Proof. By Rule AOL3, entries form a hash chain. Modifying entry k changes entry_hash[k], which breaks the prev_hash link of entry k+1. Detecting the break requires only re-computing the chain from k forward. The chain is finite by Proposition A7-F. □

3.5 Verification

The AOL is verifiable in three ways:

1. Chain integrity — recompute hashes, confirm chain (Proposition A7-H).
2. Signature integrity — verify each SK-1024 signature.
3. Semantic integrity — replay transitions, confirm state matches recorded hashes.

All three are deterministic. All three are bounded.

3.6 Storage Bound

Rule AOL5 (Storage Bound). The AOL is bounded by N_log_max, a declared constant.

When the bound is reached, the deployment must choose:

· Halt — refuse new tasks until the log is archived.
· Archive — move the oldest entries to external persistent storage (if present), preserving the hash chain.
· Compress — collapse a range of entries into a single checkpoint entry with the same hash chain link.

Compression is permitted only if the checkpoint entry is signed by the same authority as the original entries. Compression never loses the ability to verify chain integrity.

---

4. THE BOUNDED WORKING SET

4.1 Definition

Definition 4 (Bounded Working Set). The BWS is a fixed-size sequence of the most recent W state observations:

```
BWS = ⟨ o_1, o_2, …, o_W ⟩
```

where W is the window size (A4 §2.1), and each o_i is a state projection — a declared subset of the state schema.

4.2 The Update Rule

Rule BWS1 (Deterministic Push). At each FSM step, the new observation is pushed to the front of the BWS. The oldest observation falls off the end.

```
BWS' = ⟨ new_observation, o_1, …, o_{W-1} ⟩
```

Rule BWS2 (Fixed Size). |BWS| = W at all times after initialization.

Rule BWS3 (No Random Access Writes). The BWS is not addressable for writes. Only the push operation modifies it.

4.3 The Observation Schema

Definition 5 (Observation). An observation is a projection of state:

```
observation = project(state, observation_scope)
```

where observation_scope is declared in ROM. The scope is fixed per deployment.

Rule BWS4 (Declared Scope). No observation contains fields outside observation_scope.

4.4 Determinism of the BWS

Proposition A7-B (Determinism). Given a fixed input sequence, the BWS is uniquely determined at every step.

Proof. By Rule BWS1, each update is a deterministic function of the current state and the previous BWS. The state is uniquely determined by the input sequence (Corollary 5 of the theorem). By induction, the BWS is uniquely determined. □

4.5 The Memory Bounded Property

Proposition A7-B2 (Boundedness). |BWS| = W < ∞ at all times.

Proof. By Rule BWS2. □

---

5. INTERACTION WITH OTHER SPECS

5.1 A4 — State

The BWS is a component of state (A4 §2.1, s_mem). Its update is part of the state transition function δ. A4's finiteness of S depends on the BWS being bounded; A7 establishes this.

5.2 A1 — FSM

The FSM appends to the AOL at declared states:

FSM state AOL record
After COMMIT StateTransition
After PLAN Checkpoint
At REFUSE_* Refusal
At ERROR_* Error
During Phase 1 of A6 InvocationStart
During Phase 5 of A6 InvocationEnd

Every FSM terminal state (POWER_DOWN, REFUSE_, ERROR_) is preceded by an AOL append.

5.3 A2 — Actions

Every action commit produces one StateTransition record. The record contains the action ID, the pre-state hash, and the post-state hash. This is the action's audit trail.

5.4 A3 — Plans

Rollback (A3 §7.3) uses AOL entries labeled Checkpoint as rollback targets. Checkpoints are recorded at plan boundaries.

5.5 A5 — VERIFY

VERIFY failures produce Refusal records. The record carries the reason code (0x-PRECONDITION-UNMET, 0x-ENVELOPE-VIOLATED, 0x-INVARIANT-VIOLATED).

5.6 A6 — Invocation

The invocation protocol (A6 §4.1) logs at Phase 1 and Phase 5. The log carries caller_id, callee_id, context_hash, and the return status.

---

6. FORMAL PROPERTIES

The properties A7 contributes to the Verifiable Agency Theorem:

Property A7.1 (AOL Finiteness). At any time step, |AOL| < ∞.

Proof. Proposition A7-F. □

Property A7.2 (BWS Finiteness). |BWS| = W < ∞.

Proof. Proposition A7-B2. □

Property A7.3 (Memory Determinism). Given a fixed input sequence, both AOL and BWS are uniquely determined.

Proof. AOL: each append is triggered by a declared FSM state and uses the current state as input; the state is deterministic (Corollary 5). BWS: Proposition A7-B. □

Property A7.4 (Tamper Evidence). Any AOL modification is detectable.

Proof. Proposition A7-H. □

Property A7.5 (Rollback Support). The AOL supports rollback to any recorded checkpoint.

Proof. Checkpoints are AOL entries with state_hash. Rollback = restore state from state_hash. The action is a declared action, subject to VERIFY (A5 §4.2). □

These are the A7 obligations. The Verifiable Agency Theorem's assumption about the memory model is grounded.

